        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: What position does Friedrich II. von Parsberg currently or formerly hold?\n\nAnswer A: diocesan bishop\n  about: Friedrich II. von Parsberg\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1437-1461\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Friedrich II. von Parsberg was appointed as the Bishop of Eichstätt in 1437, a diocese located in the Kingdom of Bavaria. As the diocesan bishop, he was responsible for the spiritual and administrative leadership of the diocese.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Count of Astarac\n  about: Friedrich II. von Parsberg\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1520 to 1535\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In 1520, Friedrich II. von Parsberg was appointed as the Count of Astarac by King Francis I of France.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Friedrich II. von Parsberg\n\nFriedrich II. von Parsberg (died 1461) was a German prince-bishop who held the position of diocesan bishop. Early Life and Career\n\nFriedrich II. von Parsberg was born into a noble family in the 14th century. Little is known about his early life, but it is recorded that he came from a prominent family in the region of Parsberg, Bavaria. Episcopal Career\n\nFriedrich II. von Parsberg was appointed as the Bishop of Eichstätt in 1437, a diocese located in the Kingdom of Bavaria. As the diocesan bishop, he was responsible for the spiritual and administrative leadership of the diocese, which included the cities of Eichstätt, Weißenburg, and Ellingen. According to historical records, Friedrich II. von Parsberg played an active role in the governance of his diocese. He was involved in the reform of the clergy and the promotion of education within his diocese. He also worked to strengthen the relationship between the diocese and the secular authorities, including the Duchy of Bavaria. Evidence of Diocesan Bishopric\n\nFriedrich II. von Parsberg's position as diocesan bishop is supported by multiple sources, including:\n\n* The Catholic Encyclopedia, which lists Friedrich II. von Parsberg as the Bishop of Eichstätt from 1437 to 1461. [1]\n* The German Wikipedia article on Friedrich II. von Parsberg, which describes him as the \"Bischof von Eichstätt\" (Bishop of Eichstätt) during the same period. [2]\n* The website of the Diocese of Eichstätt, which includes Friedrich II. von Parsberg in its list of past bishops. [3]\n\nThese sources confirm that Friedrich II. von Parsberg held the position of diocesan bishop, with responsibility for the pastoral care and governance of the Diocese of Eichstätt. References:\n\n[1] The Catholic Encyclopedia. (1913). Eichstätt. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/en.wikisource.org\\/wiki\\/Catholic_Encyclopedia_(1913)\\/Eichstätt>\n\n[2] German Wikipedia. (2022). Friedrich II. von Parsberg. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/de.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Friedrich_II._von_Parsberg>\n\n[3] Diocese of Eichstätt.\nDocument 2: Friedrich II. von Parsberg\n\nFriedrich II. von Parsberg (1480-1543) was a German prince-bishop who held the position of Count of Astarac from 1520 to 1535. Early Life and Career\n\nFriedrich II. von Parsberg was born in 1480 in Parsberg, Bavaria, to a noble family. He was educated at the University of Ingolstadt, where he studied theology and canon law. In 1505, he was appointed as a canon of the Augsburg Cathedral, and in 1510, he became the provost of the cathedral chapter. Rise to Power\n\nIn 1515, Friedrich II. von Parsberg was elected as the prince-bishop of Augsburg, a position he held until 1520. During his tenure, he implemented several reforms, including the establishment of a new seminary and the promotion of education among the clergy. Count of Astarac\n\nIn 1520, Friedrich II. von Parsberg was appointed as the Count of Astarac by King Francis I of France. This appointment was made possible through the Treaty of Paris, signed in 1519, which established an alliance between the Holy Roman Empire and the Kingdom of France. Studies have shown that Friedrich II. von Parsberg's appointment as Count of Astarac was a strategic move by the French monarchy to strengthen its influence in the region. According to historian Dr. Helmut König, \"Friedrich II. von Parsberg's experience as a prince-bishop and his connections with the Holy Roman Empire made him an ideal candidate for the position of Count of Astarac.\" (König, 2001)\n\nDuring his tenure as Count of Astarac, Friedrich II. von Parsberg implemented several reforms, including the establishment of a new administrative system and the promotion of trade and commerce in the region. References\n\n* König, H. (2001). Friedrich II. von Parsberg: A Study of his Life and Career. Augsburg: University of Augsburg Press. * Müller, J. (1995). The Treaty of Paris: A Study of its Impact on European Politics. Paris: University of Paris Press. * Schmid, P. (2010). The Counts of Astarac: A History of the Region. Toulouse: University of Toulouse Press.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
